129-176: Consider adopting Result pattern for error handling
The coding guidelines specify preferring the @praha/byethrow Result type over traditional try-catch for functional error handling. Operations like JSON parsing should use Result.try():
- try {
-   const parsed = JSON.parse(line) as unknown;
-   const result = usageDataSchema.safeParse(parsed);
-   if (!result.success) {
-     return null;
-   }
+ const parseResult = Result.try(() => JSON.parse(line) as unknown);
+ if (Result.isFailure(parseResult)) {
+   return null;
+ }
+ 
+ const validationResult = usageDataSchema.safeParse(parseResult.value);
+ if (!validationResult.success) {
+   return null;
+ }
The file I/O operations can keep traditional try-catch since they involve "complex error handling" as mentioned in the guidelines. However, operations like JSON parsing would benefit from the Result pattern for more functional error handling.



